<RelActCalcAuto version="0">
	<Description>Uranium enrichment (U-235, U-238, U-234) from high-resolution HPGe spectra (FWHM under about 1 keV near 100 keV) - planar, broad-energy, or fine-gain coaxial - using only the 63 to 205 keV region, so it also suits spectra that end near 300 keV.
U-238 is measured from the Th-234 92.4/92.8 keV doublet, which sits among the uranium and thorium K x-rays, and from the Th-234 63.29 keV line; x-ray peaks are modelled with their natural (Lorentzian) widths, and uranium and lead fluorescence x-rays are fit as their own sources.  Each ROI lists the lines it must contain; its extent is computed from the detector's peak width.
The 63.29 keV line is below every U-235 line used, so its efficiency is extrapolated: it improves results for most planar detectors, but if the detector efficiency used does not match the detector's below about 80 keV, it can bias low-enrichment results high by several percent - removing its ROI then helps.
Uses the 'Physical' model for the relative efficiency curve, with uranium self-attenuation and an external (cadmium) absorber; a detector efficiency close to your detector's is best.
Not for items behind steel or other shielding, which removes this energy region; peaks with strong low-energy tails (e.g., from very high count rates) also degrade the result.</Description>
